<p style="text-align: center;"><img src="/images/2016/Winter-Dawn-Large.jpg" alt="Winter Dawn. 11x14, oil on linen panel. 2016" width="550" height="433" /></p>
<p style="text-align: center;">Winter Dawn. 11x14, oil on linen panel. 2016</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">As the title of this post suggests, I've started to use some of the sketches I have on hand to create bigger paintings.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;"><span style="line-height: 1.42857;">In this case I went from a 5x7 sketch to an 11x14 painting, which felt like a comfortable step. As the painting scaled up, some of the areas that had little detail in the sketch started to look too bare. This was a problem mainly in the foreground so it was fairly easy to add some extra texture to give more interest.</span></p>
<p style="text-align: left;"><span style="line-height: 1.42857;">I mixed far too much paint, and had enough left over after finishing that was able to use it to create another small painting. I used one of my plein air sketches as the starting point and painted it in a winter setting.</span></p>
